{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Field-Based Transfer and Coaching","title":"Transferring Online Cases to Field Placements","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting transferring online cases to field placements to field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"01","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/06/08"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_7_1_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_7_1_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_7_1_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_7_1_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Transferring Online Cases to Field Placements\n\n![Transferring Online Cases to Field Placements textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/07-01-transferring-online-cases-to-field-placeme.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAn online case is a controlled environment. The roster is fictional, the devices are assumed, the time is generous, and the messy variables of a real room have been edited out so you can practice one idea cleanly. A field placement removes all of that protection at once. The deepfake-detection lesson that earned full marks online may collide with a half-cart of Chromebooks, three IEP accommodations, and a bell that rings in 43 minutes. Transferring a case is not copying it; it is identifying which features of the case were essential to the learning goal and which were artifacts of the simulation, then rebuilding the lesson around your actual constraints.\n\nThis section treats the field-placement transfer note as the document where that distinction becomes visible. A strong note shows that you can tell a portable instructional idea from a setting-specific assumption, and that you adjusted the second without abandoning the first.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Separate the transferable instructional goal of an online case from the setting-specific assumptions baked into the simulation.\n- Map a fictional case roster onto a real placement roster, including device ratios, accommodations, and home-access constraints.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with the suggestions you accepted and the ones you rejected as invented facts.\n- Write a transfer note a mentor teacher can audit: original case, real constraint, specific change, and evidence the change worked.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA case study is a teaching argument with the friction removed. When the module said \"a class of 30 with reliable home laptops,\" that detail was not neutral; it silently licensed every design choice that followed, including the take-home reflection and the whole-class debate format. The transfer question is which of those choices survive contact with your placement.\n\nThe danger is treating the grade you earned online as proof the lesson is ready to teach. The grade certifies that you reasoned well inside the simulation's assumptions. It says nothing about whether those assumptions hold in your Tuesday placement. The transfer note exists to surface the gap between the two settings before students pay for it.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nTransferring a case turns on one move: decomposing the original lesson into a transferable core and a set of enabling conditions. The transferable core is the learning goal and the reasoning sequence that serves it (in the deepfake case, having students argue from evidence about a manipulated artifact). The enabling conditions are the resources the simulation provided for free: one device per student, dependable home connectivity, no accommodations, a class size that fits a single discussion circle.\n\nWhen you move to a placement, the core should stay fixed while the enabling conditions are renegotiated against reality. This is the practical content of the [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) idea that technology, pedagogy, and content are interdependent: change the technology context (14 devices instead of 30) and the pedagogy must change with it (stations instead of whole-class), or the content goal silently degrades.\n\nThree lenses keep the renegotiation honest:\n\n- **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** asks whether the lesson's technological demand still fits the learning goal once the device reality changes, or whether you are now teaching the tool instead of the content.\n- **Critical edtech studies** asks who the original assumptions excluded. \"Reliable home laptops\" is not a neutral default; it encodes a class assumption that the take-home step quietly enforces.\n- **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** asks what a generative tool will do when you ask it to adapt the case. It tends to restore the convenient assumptions (one-to-one devices, named cooperative students) because those make for a cleaner narrative.\n\n> **Recent evidence:** [NCES (2021)](https://nces.ed.gov/programs/coe/indicator/cch/home-internet-access) reports that 6% of children in the lowest income quartile lacked home internet versus 1% in the highest, with cost the main barrier for lower-income families. The scenario's nine-of-28 figure is an instance of this documented homework gap, not just a narrative number (see [Selwyn](https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160) on critical edtech).\n\nThe test of whether a lens did real work is simple: it should have changed a concrete feature of your lesson (a format, a grouping, a dropped step), not just appeared in your rationale.\n\n<inline-check question=\"The online case specified 'a class of 30 with reliable home laptops.' Is that detail part of the transferable core or an enabling condition?\" explanation=\"It is an enabling condition: a resource the simulation supplied for free. The transferable core is the learning goal (argue from evidence about a manipulated artifact). Reliable home laptops is exactly the kind of assumption that must be renegotiated against your real placement.\" options='[{\"text\":\"An enabling condition the simulation supplied for free\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Part of the transferable core that must survive the move\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Proof the lesson is ready to teach because it earned full marks\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nCompare two versions of a transfer note. The thin version copies the case forward and certifies it with the online grade:\n\n> I will run the deepfake-detection debate from the module and assign the take-home reflection. It earned full marks online.\n\nThis is not a transfer note; it is a paste. It treats the simulation's enabling conditions as if they were facts about the placement. A note that actually does the work looks like this:\n\n> The module assumed 30 students, one device each, and reliable home access. My placement has 14 Chromebooks for 28 students, three students with read-aloud and extended-time accommodations, and nine students who reported no reliable home device. I kept the evidence-based debate (the core) and changed the delivery: a three-station rotation so 14 devices serve 28, the take-home reflection dropped because nine students could not complete it, and read-aloud preloaded for the three accommodated students.\n\nThe second version is not longer for its own sake. It names the original assumption, the real constraint that breaks it, and the specific structural change that preserves the learning goal anyway.\n\n### Annotated Judgment Call: Transferring the Deepfake-Detection Case\n\nWork the move on a real scenario. Maya completed an online module case in which candidates debated a deepfake-detection lesson for a fictional class of 30 with reliable home laptops. She now plans to teach it in her Tuesday placement.\n\n1. **Before (copy-over draft):** \"I will run the deepfake-detection debate from the module and assign the take-home reflection. It earned full marks online.\"\n2. **Observed constraint:** Her placement has one shared cart of 14 Chromebooks for 28 students, and 3 students have IEP accommodations for read-aloud and extended time. The fictional roster modeled none of this.\n3. **After (transfer note):** \"I converted the live whole-class debate into a 3-station rotation so 14 devices serve 28 students. I dropped the take-home step because 9 of 28 students reported no reliable home device. I preloaded the read-aloud setting for the 3 students with that accommodation.\"\n4. **Suggestion rejected:** An AI rewrite invented one-to-one laptops and named imaginary students. I removed those invented facts because they contradicted what I observed.\n5. **Evidence the change worked:** In the placement, 26 of 28 students completed the station task, versus the 9 who would have finished the take-home version based on the home-device count.\n\nThe judgment is visible: a named placement constraint drove a specific change, and the candidate can show what students did differently because of it.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen you transfer a case, resolve conflicts in this order: protect the learning goal first, satisfy the access constraint second, and preserve the original format only third. Most novice transfers invert this and protect the format (\"it has to be a whole-class debate\") at the expense of access. The deepfake case shows the correct order: the format (whole-class) was the most expendable element, the access constraint (14 devices, no home access) was non-negotiable, and the goal (argue from evidence about a manipulated artifact) was preserved by changing the format to fit the constraint.\n\nBefore you teach a transferred case, three checks should pass:\n\n1. **Core check:** Can you state the one learning goal that must survive the move, separate from how the case happened to deliver it?\n2. **Assumption check:** Which enabling conditions did the simulation provide that your placement does not, and which lesson step depends on each one?\n3. **Change check:** What did you restructure to preserve the goal under your real constraints, and what AI-suggested \"fix\" did you reject because it reintroduced an assumption you do not have?\n\nIf any check fails, you have copied the case, not transferred it.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Protect the learning goal (argue from evidence about a manipulated artifact)\", \"Satisfy the access constraint (14 devices, no home access)\", \"Preserve the original format (whole-class debate)\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThe deepfake case puts you in the room. Walk the transfer decision and watch what each move costs students.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Transferring the deepfake-detection case to your placement\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"Your online case assumed 30 students, one device each, reliable home access. Your placement has 14 Chromebooks for 28 students and 9 students with no reliable home device. What do you do first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Run the whole-class debate and assign the take-home reflection as designed\",\"next\":\"paste\",\"feedback\":\"You protected the format first, inverting the priority order.\"},{\"label\":\"State the learning goal that must survive, separate from how the case delivered it\",\"next\":\"core\",\"feedback\":\"You protected the learning goal first, the correct priority.\"},{\"label\":\"Ask an AI tool to adapt the case for you and run whatever it returns\",\"next\":\"ai\",\"feedback\":\"You outsourced the decomposition before doing it yourself.\"}]},\"paste\":{\"prompt\":\"The take-home reflection is assigned. What happens to the 9 students without home devices?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"They cannot complete it; the lesson silently fails them\",\"next\":\"fail\",\"feedback\":\"This is feature transfer: the format survived, the goal eroded.\"}]},\"fail\":{\"prompt\":\"You copied the case rather than transferring it. Restart and protect the goal first.\",\"feedback\":\"Result: 9 of 28 excluded. The format was the most expendable element, not the access constraint.\",\"choices\":[]},\"core\":{\"prompt\":\"Goal fixed: argue from evidence about a manipulated artifact. The format and home step still assume resources you lack. What change preserves the goal?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Convert the debate to a three-station rotation so 14 devices serve 28, drop the take-home step\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"You renegotiated the enabling conditions while keeping the core fixed.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep the take-home step but tell students to find a device somehow\",\"next\":\"fail\",\"feedback\":\"This pushes the access barrier onto the 9 students instead of redesigning around it.\"}]},\"ai\":{\"prompt\":\"The AI rewrite hands you one-to-one laptops and named cooperative students. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Use it; the narrative is cleaner than your messy roster\",\"next\":\"fail\",\"feedback\":\"It restored convenient assumptions you do not have.\"},{\"label\":\"Reject the invented facts and decompose the case yourself first\",\"next\":\"core\",\"feedback\":\"You caught the AI reintroducing assumptions and did the core work yourself.\"}]},\"win\":{\"prompt\":\"26 of 28 completed the station task, versus the 9 who could have finished the take-home version. A mentor can audit the change.\",\"feedback\":\"You transferred the case: named the assumption, named the constraint that broke it, made the specific change that preserved the goal.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nThe most common error is reading the online grade as a clearance to teach. It is not; it certifies reasoning inside the simulation, not fit with your placement. A second error is \"feature transfer,\" where the candidate preserves the surface format (the debate, the take-home) and lets the learning goal quietly erode because the format no longer fits the room. A third is trusting an AI adaptation that restores convenient assumptions; when a rewrite hands you one-to-one devices and cooperative named students, it has optimized for a tidy story, not your roster.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the decomposition to a fresh case. Suppose an online module taught a collaborative-annotation lesson where 24 students mark up a shared document in real time, each on a laptop. The transferable core is collaborative close reading where students make their thinking visible to each other. The enabling conditions are one device per student and a platform the district has approved.\n\nNow drop it into a placement with 12 tablets and a district that has not vetted the annotation tool. The core survives only if you change the conditions: pair students so 12 devices serve 24, and substitute a printed shared text with a document camera for the platform the district will not allow. If you instead keep the original platform because the case used it, you have let an enabling condition override the learning goal and the access reality at once. The walkthrough is the same every time: name the core, list what the simulation gave you for free, and rebuild only the conditions while leaving the core untouched.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a case, keep a two-column note: **transferable core** on the left, **simulation assumption** on the right. Every detail in the case belongs in one column. \"Argue from evidence about a manipulated image\" is core. \"Reliable home laptops\" is assumption. The discipline of sorting forces you to notice the conditions the case smuggled in as if they were features.\n\nA strong reader of a transferable case can answer four questions:\n\n1. What single learning goal is this case really teaching, stripped of its delivery format?\n2. Which enabling condition, if removed, would break the lesson the fastest?\n3. Which condition encodes an equity assumption that excludes some of my actual students?\n4. What would I rebuild if my device ratio, home-access reality, or accommodation profile changed again?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA transfer note is strong when a mentor teacher, reading only the note, can reconstruct both the original case and your placement without you in the room. That means the note states the case's assumptions explicitly (not just its activities), names the specific constraint that breaks each assumption, and shows the structural change that preserved the goal. Polish (clean formatting, a tidy table) is the last layer; if the note reads smoothly but cannot tell the reader what the simulation assumed, it has skipped the layer that matters.\n\nA useful closing paragraph sounds like this: \"I changed the delivery of this case because the simulation assumed resources my placement does not have, and copying it forward would have failed the nine students without home devices. I rejected the AI rewrite that restored one-to-one laptops, because looking adaptable is not the same as being teachable in this room.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same deepfake lesson to a different placement: a one-to-one laptop school, but with a strict district policy banning any tool that uploads student-created media to an outside server. Now the device constraint disappears and the take-home step becomes viable again, but a new enabling condition (free upload of media for analysis) breaks. The transferable core has not moved; the lesson still argues from evidence about a manipulated artifact. What changes is which condition you renegotiate. If you can re-derive the lesson under this different broken assumption rather than reusing your station rotation out of habit, you understand transfer rather than one specific fix.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/07-01-transferring-online-cases-to-field-placeme-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"07.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the field-placement transfer note connecting tool choice to classroom constraints for CAT 531 07.01. A peer comment points to an evidence-move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the transfer note states the original case assumptions, names the placement constraint that breaks each one, shows the structural change that preserved the learning goal, and records the AI suggestion you rejected.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching\", \"cat531_supplement_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). Mishra & Koehler (2006). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. Selwyn. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017). Perrotta & Selwyn (2019).\n- AI-TPACK. Ning et al. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- NCES (2021). Children's internet access at home, Condition of Education. https://nces.ed.gov/programs/coe/indicator/cch/home-internet-access\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_1_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements","stem":"An online module case asked candidates to debate a deepfake-detection lesson with no real students present. A candidate wants to transfer it to her Tuesday placement. Which sentence in her transfer note shows defensible judgment rather than a copy-over?","options":["I will run the same deepfake-detection debate I completed online because it earned a high grade in the module.","The online case assumed reliable home devices; my placement has one shared cart for 28 students, so I will convert the live debate into a station rotation and drop the take-home step.","I will keep every step identical so my mentor can see I followed the online module exactly.","I will make the slides look more professional before I bring the case to the placement."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer is defensible when the candidate names the placement constraint (one shared device cart) that the online case never faced and changes the activity to fit it. The other options copy the case over or treat polish as the decision.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_1_context_identical_transfer","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_1_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"The online case used a fictional roster with no IEPs. Which detail about the real placement most changes how the candidate must adapt the case before teaching it?","options":["The placement classroom has a brighter projector than the online demo showed.","Three of the 28 students have IEP accommodations for extended time and read-aloud, which the fictional roster never modeled.","The candidate prefers the font used in the original online slides.","The mentor teacher also took CAT 531 a few years ago."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Real accommodations the fictional case ignored are the constraint that forces redesign. The other details are cosmetic or irrelevant to the transfer decision.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_1_fictional_case_equals_real_class","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_1_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"The candidate asks an AI tool to rewrite the online case for her real placement. The AI invents a class size, names students, and assumes one-to-one laptops. What is the defensible next move?","options":["Submit the AI rewrite because it is fluent and already names students.","Treat the rewrite as a draft, then correct the invented class size, device access, and student details against what she actually observed in the placement.","Avoid AI entirely because it does not know the placement.","Ask the AI to add more detail until the case sounds realistic."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The AI cannot know the real placement, so its invented facts must be checked against the candidate's own observation before the case can transfer. Fluency is not faithfulness to the field context.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_1_ai_invented_context_as_fact","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_1_transferring_online_cases_to_field_placements_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"After teaching the transferred case, the candidate's mentor asks how she knows the adaptation worked in this placement. Which evidence best answers that question?","options":["The online module gave the original case a passing score.","A side-by-side note showing the online assumption, the placement constraint she observed, the change she made, and what students did differently because of it.","The lesson finished within the class period.","Students said the activity was fun."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Defensible transfer evidence connects the original assumption, the observed constraint, the change, and the student outcome. A module grade, timing, or enjoyment alone does not show the adaptation served learning in this context.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_1_module_grade_proves_field_fit","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"3ec00fdaa18c90455552d8e50baee4248922278821d952099ff24e73dca50308","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}